      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mumford was named after Jesse Mumford, who secured a ferry license on the Brazos in 1855.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mumford%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1890, Mumford boasted a gin, lumberyard, brick kiln, syrup mill, broom factory, church, and school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/mumford-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hearne and Brazos Valley Railway reached Mumford in 1891, linking it to regional growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/mumford-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1895, the first iron bridge crossed the Brazos near Mumford, connecting Robertson and Burleson counties.",
